'''Helena Grace Buonanno Foulkes''' ({{IPAc-en|ˈ|f|oʊ|k|s}}; née '''Buonanno'''; born July 18, 1964) is an American businesswoman and politician. She was the [[chief executive officer]] of [[Hudson's Bay Company]] and a marketing executive at [[CVS Pharmacy]]. A member of the [[Democratic Party (United States)|Democratic Party]], Foulkes unsuccessfully ran for [[governor of Rhode Island]] in [[2022 Rhode Island gubernatorial election|2022]] before winning the nomination in [[2026 Rhode Island gubernatorial election|2026]], both times challenging incumbent governor [[Dan McKee]] for the Democratic nomination.<ref>{{Cite web |date=September 9, 2026 |title=Rhode Island Governor primary results |url=https://apnews.com/projects/elections-2026/rhode-island-primary-results-governor/#Dem |website=AP News}}</ref>

==Early life and education==
Buonanno was born in [[Providence, Rhode Island]], on July 18, 1964.<ref>{{Cite web |last=NEWS |first=NBC 10 |date=2022-07-29 |title=Beyond The Podium: Foulkes says she knows how to attract top talent to solve problems |url=https://turnto10.com/politics/kyc/rhode-island-governor/beyond-the-podium-helena-foulkes-cvs-education-obamacare-fdr-democratic-nomination-rhode-island-governor-race-elections-decision-2022 |access-date=2026-08-23 |website=WJAR |language=en}}</ref> She is the eldest of five children of Bernard and Martha Buonanno (née Dodd).<ref>{{Cite news |date=1989-07-23 |title=Helena Buonanno Weds W. G. Foulkes |url=https://www.nytimes.com/1989/07/23/style/helena-buonanno-weds-w-g-foulkes.html |access-date=2026-02-08 |work=The New York Times |language=en-US |issn=0362-4331}}</ref><ref name="auto">{{Cite news |date=1995-12-17 |title=WEDDINGS;Ms. Buonanno And Mr. Chase |url=https://www.nytimes.com/1995/12/17/style/weddings-ms-buonanno-and-mr-chase.html |access-date=2026-02-08 |work=The New York Times |language=en-US |issn=0362-4331}}</ref> She is the maternal granddaughter of Connecticut senator [[Thomas J. Dodd]] and the niece of Connecticut senator and 2008 presidential candidate [[Chris Dodd]].<ref name="auto"/> She was born to an Italian and Irish Catholic family.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.mainepublic.org/2026-09-09/helena-foulkes-projected-to-defeat-rhode-island-gov-dan-mckee|title=Helena Foulkes ousts Gov. Dan McKee in a rare Rhode Island primary defeat|first=Isabella|last=Jibilian|date=September 10, 2026|website=Maine Public}}</ref>

Buonanno was raised in [[Rhode Island]] and attended Lincoln School in [[Providence, Rhode Island|Providence]]. She earned her Bachelor of Arts from [[Harvard College]], graduating ''[[magna cum laude]]'' in 1986.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Helena Foulkes to Serve as Harvard Board of Overseers President {{!}} News {{!}} The Harvard Crimson |url=https://www.thecrimson.com/article/2021/4/27/new-overseers-prez-vp/ |access-date=2026-02-08 |website=www.thecrimson.com}}</ref> While at Harvard, Buonanno's senior thesis was advised by [[Larry Summers]].<ref name="FortuneSept2015" /> She later earned her [[Master of Business Administration]] from [[Harvard Business School]].<ref name="FortuneSept2015" />

==Business career==
After graduating from Harvard, Foulkes briefly worked at [[Goldman Sachs]] and [[Tiffany & Co]] before leaving to earn her MBA. She began working at [[CVS Health|CVS]] in 1992.<ref name=FortuneSept2015/> She rose to become the company's executive vice president and chief marketing officer. She oversaw the launch of the ExtraCare card, a membership program offering savings to participants.<ref name=USNews2013/> Foulkes also created the Pharmacy Advisor program, its purpose being to offer advice to customers with chronic conditions, either in stores or over the phone.<ref name=FortuneSept2015/>

In 2011, CVS made Foulkes its chief health care strategy and marketing officer, a new position created for her.<ref name=USNews2013>{{Cite news|url=http://money.usnews.com/money/careers/articles/2013/07/16/around-the-water-cooler-with-cvs-caremarks-helena-foulkes |title=Around the Water Cooler With CVS Caremark's Helena Foulkes |work=[[U.S. News & World Report]] |first=Stephanie |last=Steinberg |date=July 16, 2013 |accessdate=September 13, 2015}}</ref> In July 2015, ''[[Fortune (magazine)|Fortune]]'' magazine reported that as part of her role, Foulkes oversaw 9,600 retail stores and 18 distribution centers.<ref>{{Cite news|url=http://fortune.com/2015/07/14/cvs-helena-foulkes/ |title=CVS Health chief: Consumers care more about wallet than health |work=[[Fortune (magazine)|Fortune]] |first=Leena |last=Rao |date=July 14, 2015 |accessdate=September 13, 2015}}</ref> She helped spur the decision to stop selling [[cigarette]]s and [[tobacco products]], citing the need for CVS to better position itself as a healthcare company.<ref name=FortuneSept2015>{{Cite news|url=http://fortune.com/2015/09/11/cvs-health-helena-foulkes/ |title=The change agent inside CVS |work=[[Fortune (magazine)|Fortune]] |first=Phil |last=Wahba |date=September 11, 2015 |accessdate=September 13, 2015}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.usatoday.com/story/news/nation/2014/02/05/cvs-caremark-tobacco/5220479/ |title=CVS Caremark to stop selling tobacco products |work=[[USA Today]] |first=Kelly |last=Kennedy |date=February 5, 2014 |accessdate=September 16, 2015}}</ref> She oversaw the company's philanthropic endeavors, and developed digital strategies to help consumers learn how to fulfill their pharmaceutical needs.<ref name=USNews2013/>

In 2015, ''Fortune'' magazine included her on its list of [[Fortune Most Powerful Women Entrepreneurs|Most Powerful Women]], citing Foulkes' role in the "$1.9 billion purchase of [[Target Corporation|Target]]'s prescription-filling business—a deal that will give CVS the most pharmacy locations in the U.S.—and launching upgraded beauty and healthy food sections in many of the stores".<ref>{{Cite news|url=http://fortune.com/most-powerful-women/helena-foulkes-14/ |title=Helena Foulkes, 51 |work=[[Fortune (magazine)|Fortune]] |date=2015 |accessdate=September 16, 2015}}</ref>

On February 5, 2018, Hudson's Bay Co., owner of [[Saks Fifth Avenue]], named Foulkes its new CEO, saying it would be effective on February 19.<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2018-02-05/saks-owner-hudson-s-bay-appoints-cvs-executive-foulkes-as-ceo|title=Saks Owner Hudson's Bay Appoints CVS Executive Helena Foulkes as CEO|date=February 5, 2018|work=Bloomberg.com|access-date=February 6, 2018|language=en}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.chainstoreage.com/breaking-news/cvs-health-exec-named-ceo-department-store-giant/|title=CVS Health exec named CEO of department store giant|date=February 5, 2018|work=Chain Store Age|access-date=February 7, 2018|publisher=Chain Store Age|language=en-US}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news|url=http://fortune.com/2018/02/05/cvs-health-hbc/|title=Hudson's Bay Poaches Helena Foulkes From CVS|work=Fortune|access-date=February 20, 2018|publisher=Fortune|language=en}}</ref> In her first six weeks as CEO, Foulkes was challenged with refashioning the company's business strategies given the industry's declining sales at the time.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://vancouversun.com/news/retail-marketing/hudsons-bay-co-posts-weak-showing-in-fourth-quarter/wcm/db9fe14e-8a94-4239-a205-f324ddc10e28|title=New Hudson's Bay CEO Helena Foulkes says 'no sacred cows' as retailer seeks elusive turnaround|last=Shaw|first=Hollie|website=Vancouver Sun|access-date=July 23, 2018}}</ref>

In March 2020, Foulkes announced that she was leaving Hudson's Bay Co. following a deal reached with shareholders to go private.<ref>{{Cite news|last=Kapner|first=Suzanne|url=https://www.wsj.com/articles/ceo-of-saks-parent-to-step-down-11583242779|title=WSJ News Exclusive {{!}} CEO of Saks Parent to Step Down|date=March 3, 2020|work=Wall Street Journal|access-date=March 4, 2020|language=en-US|issn=0099-9660}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.cnbc.com/2020/03/03/hudsons-bay-ceo-helena-foulkes-reportedly-on-the-way-out.html|title=Hudson's Bay CEO Helena Foulkes on the way out as retailer prepares to go private|last=Thomas|first=Lauren|date=March 3, 2020|website=CNBC|language=en|access-date=March 5, 2020}}</ref>

== Political career ==
{{Main|2022 Rhode Island gubernatorial election|2026 Rhode Island gubernatorial election}}
On October 13, 2021, Foulkes announced her candidacy for [[governor of Rhode Island]] in the [[2022 Rhode Island gubernatorial election|2022 election]]. [[WPRI]] reported that "she would seek to spur job growth, reduce regulations, deal with the effects of climate change, and improve public education."<ref>{{Cite web
|url=https://www.wpri.com/news/politics/helena-foulkes-plans-run-for-ri-governor/
|title= Helena Foulkes kicks off run for RI governor
|last=Nesi
|first=Ted
|work=WPRI|date= October 12, 2021
}}</ref> She faced incumbent governor [[Dan McKee]], former [[Secretary of State of Rhode Island|Secretary of State]] [[Matt Brown (American politician)|Matt Brown]], and incumbent Secretary of State [[Nellie Gorbea]] in the Democratic primary. She finished second, losing the primary to McKee by just under 3,900 votes.<ref>{{Cite web |title=RI.gov: Election Results |url=https://www.ri.gov/election/results/2022/statewide_primary/ |access-date=2026-09-14 |website=www.ri.gov}}</ref>

On September 9, 2025, Foulkes announced her candidacy for governor of Rhode Island in the [[2026 Rhode Island gubernatorial election|2026 election]].<ref>{{Cite web |last=Anderson |first=Patrick |title=Helena Foulkes launches second bid for governor. What we know. |url=https://www.providencejournal.com/story/news/politics/2025/09/09/helena-foulkes-launches-bid-to-unseat-mckee-as-ri-governor-in-2026/86055267007/ |access-date=2025-09-19 |website=The Providence Journal |language=en-US}}</ref> She again faced McKee in the Democratic primary, defeating him in a landslide. She won every municipality in the state,<ref>{{Cite news |last=Mazzei |first=Patricia |last2=Pager |first2=Tyler |last3=Rogers |first3=Katie |last4=Rogers |first4=Katie |last5=Goldmacher |first5=Shane |last6=Rogers |first6=Katie |last7=Duehren |first7=Andrew |last8=Goldmacher |first8=Shane |last9=Pager |first9=Tyler |date=2026-09-09 |title=Rhode Island Governor Primary 2026: Live Election Results, McKee vs. Foulkes |url=https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2026/09/09/us/elections/results-rhode-island-governor-primary.html |access-date=2026-09-10 |work=The New York Times |language=en-US |issn=0362-4331}}</ref> and became the first person to unseat an elected incumbent governor since 2014.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2026-09-10 |title=Rhode Island Gov. Dan McKee loses Democratic primary to businesswoman Helena Foulkes |url=https://www.nbcnews.com/politics/2026-election/helena-foulkes-winner-rhode-island-democratic-governor-primary-mckee-rcna596559 |access-date=2026-09-10 |website=NBC News |language=en}}</ref>

In the 2026 gubernatorial campaign, Foulkes has promised using the state’s millionaire's tax to fund new housing developments, an audit of increased energy bills, and to hold the RI Department of Transportation accountable for delays on construction of the Washington Bridge,<ref name=":1">{{Cite web |title=Plans |url=https://helenafoulkes.com/plans/ |access-date=2026-10-11 |website=Helena Foulkes for Governor |language=en-US}}</ref> She has also promised to raise Medicaid reimbursement rates, and create an employer-matched childcare fund.<ref name=":1" />.Foulkes has received endorsements from, among others, Speaker Emeritus Nancy Pelosi, the RI Association of Democratic City and Town Chairs, the Rhode Island SIEU State Council, and Climate Action Rhode Island.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Endorsements |url=https://helenafoulkes.com/endorsements/ |access-date=2026-10-11 |website=Helena Foulkes for Governor |language=en-US}}</ref>

==Personal life==
Through her mother Martha, Foulkes is a member of the Dodd family. Her grandfather [[Thomas J. Dodd]] was a prosecutor during the Nuremberg Trials and later a senator from Connecticut, her uncle [[Chris Dodd]] was a former U.S. senator and the partial namesake of the [[Dodd–Frank Wall Street Reform and Consumer Protection Act|Dodd-Frank Act]], while her uncle Thomas was a [[Ambassadors of the United States|U.S. ambassador]] under the [[Presidency of Bill Clinton|Clinton administration]].<ref name="NYTwedding">{{Cite news |date=July 23, 1989 |title=Helena Buonanno Weds W. G. Foulkes |url=https://www.nytimes.com/1989/07/23/style/helena-buonanno-weds-w-g-foulkes.html |accessdate=September 16, 2015 |work=[[The New York Times]]}}</ref>

Foulkes married William Foulkes in 1989.<ref name="NYTwedding" /> The two met while students at Harvard and have four children together.<ref name="FortuneSept2015" />

From 2016 to 2022, Foulkes served on the [[Harvard Board of Overseers]], the university's second-highest governing body. She was the board's president from 2021 to 2022.<ref name="210427HC">{{Cite web |last=Goodman |first=Jasper G |date=April 27, 2021 |title=Helena Foulkes to Serve as Harvard Board of Overseers President |url=https://www.thecrimson.com/article/2021/4/27/new-overseers-prez-vp/ |access-date=April 27, 2021 |work=Harvard Crimson |quote=Helena Buonanno Foulkes '86, a former corporate executive who has held top positions at CVS Health and served as CEO of the Hudson’s Bay Company, will serve as the president of Harvard’s Board of Overseers for the next year, the University announced Monday.}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=Harvard Board of Overseers President Helena Foulkes '86 Announces Bid For Rhode Island Governor {{!}} News {{!}} The Harvard Crimson |url=https://www.thecrimson.com/article/2021/10/19/helena-foulkes-governor-bid/ |access-date=2026-02-08 |website=www.thecrimson.com}}</ref>

